YACC(1)                        P32W Unix                         YACC(1)

NAME
       yacc - a parser generator (LALR(1), POSIX)

SYNOPSIS
       yacc [-dltv] [-b prefix] [-p symprefix] grammar

DESCRIPTION
       yacc reads a grammar and writes y.tab.c, a C program whose
       function yyparse() parses its input by the grammar, running
       the C action of each rule as the rule is recognised. The
       input comes a token at a time from yylex(), which the user
       writes (by hand, or with lex(1)). The parser is an LALR(1)
       automaton in compressed tables, and compiles with cc(1).

       A grammar has three parts, separated by lines of %%:

              declarations
              %%
              rules
              %%
              C code (optional; copied to the end of y.tab.c)

       Comments /* ... */ and // may appear anywhere outside the
       copied code. Names are letters, digits, _ and . (not starting
       with a digit). A character in single quotes ('+', '\n',
       '\\', '\'', '\033') is a token whose number is its code.

DECLARATIONS
       %{ ... %}          C code, copied as it is to the head of
                          y.tab.c (#include lines, declarations)
       %token [<t>] NAME [n] ...
                          tokens; a number n after a name sets
                          its token number (else 257 on, in order)
       %left [<t>] T ...  tokens of one precedence, grouping to
       %right [<t>] T ... the left, to the right, or not at all;
       %nonassoc [<t>] T ...
                          each line binds more tightly than the
                          lines before it
       %type <t> name ... the type of nonterminals (or tokens)
       %start name        the start symbol (else the first rule's)
       %union { ... }     the value type: YYSTYPE becomes
                          typedef union YYSTYPE { ... } YYSTYPE

       Without %union, values are int (or the YYSTYPE that a
       #define in %{ %} names). The old forms %term, %binary,
       %< %> %2 %0 %= and \ for % are accepted too.

RULES
       A rule is a nonterminal, a colon, and alternatives separated
       by |, ending with ; (which may be left out):

              expr : expr '+' expr      { $$ = $1 + $3; }
                   | '(' expr ')'       { $$ = $2; }
                   | NUMBER
                   ;

       An alternative may be empty. %prec T at the end of one gives
       it the precedence of token T (else it has that of its last
       token). An action { ... } is C; in it $$ is the value of the
       rule (by default $1, the first symbol's), $1 ... $n the
       values of its symbols, $0 and $-1 ... those of the symbols
       before the rule on the parser's stack, and $<t>$, $<t>n give
       a value with the member t of the union. When %union or a
       <type> is used, every $ must have a type: the symbol's
       (%token <t>, %type <t>) or an explicit $<t>.

       An action in the middle of a rule runs when the parser gets
       to it: yacc makes it an empty rule of its own ($$1 : ;) in
       its place. It counts as a symbol of the rule: the value it
       sets with $<t>$ is $<t>n in a later action (n its place).

       The token error matches where the parser can resume after a
       syntax error (see ERRORS).

THE PARSER
       int yyparse(void)    parses the whole input: 0 if it was
                            accepted, 1 if not (an error it could
                            not recover from, or YYABORT), 2 when
                            the stack (YYMAXDEPTH, 500) overflows
       int yylex(void)      the user's: returns the next token's
                            number (0 or less at the end) and sets
                            yylval to its value
       yyerror(char *msg)   the user's (or liby's): called with
                            "syntax error" (or "yacc stack
                            overflow"); its result is not used
       yylval, yychar       the token's value and the lookahead
       yynerrs              the number of errors reported
       yydebug              with -t: nonzero traces the parse on
                            the standard error

       In actions: YYACCEPT and YYABORT return from yyparse (0, 1);
       YYERROR starts error recovery as if the token were wrong
       (without a message); yyerrok ends recovery at once,
       yyclearin drops the lookahead token; YYRECOVERING() is
       nonzero while recovering.

       y.tab.c declares neither yylex nor yyerror: declare them in
       %{ %} (or let cc warn of an implicit declaration).

ERRORS
       On a token that no rule allows, the parser calls yyerror
       (unless still recovering from an error), then pops states
       until one where error can be shifted, shifts it, and goes on;
       tokens that cannot follow are dropped, and three tokens must
       be shifted before another message is given. A rule such as

              line : error '\n'    { yyerrok; }

       so skips to the end of the line. With no such state, or at
       the end of the input, yyparse returns 1.

CONFLICTS
       Where the grammar is not LALR(1), yacc chooses: a reduction
       against a shift by the precedence of the rule and of the
       token, if both have one (the higher wins; at the same level
       %left reduces, %right shifts and %nonassoc makes it an
       error); otherwise the shift, and between reductions the
       earlier rule. The choices without precedence are counted on
       the standard error, as

              yacc: 1 shift/reduce conflict

       and are listed with each state in y.output (-v), with the
       ones precedence settled.

OPTIONS
       -d     also write y.tab.h: the tokens' #defines, YYSTYPE and
              extern YYSTYPE yylval, for a separate lexer
       -l     leave out the #line lines that relate y.tab.c to the
              grammar
       -t     compile the trace in (YYDEBUG 1; set yydebug to use
              it); else cc -DYYDEBUG=1 does
       -v     also write y.output: the grammar's numbered rules,
              each state's items and actions, the conflicts
       -b prefix
              write prefix.tab.c (.tab.h, .output) instead of y.*
       -p symprefix
              symprefix instead of yy in yyparse, yylex, yyerror,
              yylval, yychar, yydebug and yynerrs (by #define), so
              that two parsers can be in one program

LIBRARY
       cc ... -ly links liby (/usr/lib/liby.a), with POSIX's main
       (it returns yyparse()) and a yyerror that writes the message
       and a newline on the standard error. Either may be the
       program's own.

EXAMPLES
       A calculator, calc.y:

              %{
              #include <stdio.h>
              #include <ctype.h>
              int yylex(void);
              int yyerror(char *s);
              %}
              %token NUMBER
              %left '+' '-'
              %left '*' '/'
              %right UMINUS
              %%
              lines : /* empty */
                    | lines expr '\n'   { printf("%d\n", $2); }
                    | lines error '\n'  { yyerrok; }
                    ;
              expr  : expr '+' expr     { $$ = $1 + $3; }
                    | expr '-' expr     { $$ = $1 - $3; }
                    | expr '*' expr     { $$ = $1 * $3; }
                    | expr '/' expr     { $$ = $3 ? $1 / $3 : 0; }
                    | '-' expr %prec UMINUS { $$ = -$2; }
                    | '(' expr ')'      { $$ = $2; }
                    | NUMBER
                    ;
              %%
              int yylex(void)
              {
                  int c = getchar();
                  while (c == ' ')
                      c = getchar();
                  if (isdigit(c)) {
                      yylval = 0;
                      for (; isdigit(c); c = getchar())
                          yylval = yylval * 10 + c - '0';
                      ungetc(c, stdin);
                      return NUMBER;
                  }
                  return c == EOF ? 0 : c;
              }

       Make it and use it (main and yyerror from liby):

              $ yacc calc.y
              $ cc -o calc y.tab.c -ly
              $ echo '2 * (3 + 4) - -1' | ./calc
              15

       yacc -d gram.y; lex scan.l; cc y.tab.c lex.yy.c -ly -ll
       builds a parser with a lexer from lex(1) (scan.l includes
       y.tab.h for the token numbers).

FILES
       y.tab.c             the parser
       y.tab.h             its tokens (-d)
       y.output            the states (-v)
       /usr/lib/liby.a     main and yyerror (-ly)

DIAGNOSTICS
       Errors in the grammar name the file and the line; yacc stops
       at the first in the declarations or in a rule's syntax, and
       at the end of the rules for the others (a symbol used but
       neither a token nor with rules, a $n with no type or past
       the end of the rule, a token on the left of a rule). The
       conflicts and rules never reduced are counted; a nonterminal
       that derives no string of tokens is a warning (an error for
       the start symbol). No output is written after an error.

EXIT STATUS
       0 (even with conflicts); 1 for an error in the grammar, a
       usage error, or a file that cannot be read or written.

SEE ALSO
       lex(1), cc(1); S. C. Johnson, Yacc: Yet Another
       Compiler-Compiler (1975); F. DeRemer and T. Pennello,
       Efficient Computation of LALR(1) Look-Ahead Sets (1982),
       the method yacc uses.

BUGS
       String literals as tokens ("<="), %expect, %define, @n
       locations, %destructor and the other bison extensions are
       not there. Token numbers must be below 65536.

       yyparse keeps its stacks (YYMAXDEPTH entries: #define it in
       %{ %} for more) on the C stack and never grows them; it is
       not reentrant (yylval, yychar and yynerrs are global).

       P32W is slow: yacc takes 2 or 3 seconds for a small grammar
       and 20 for one the size of C's (213 rules, 352 states), -v
       10 more; cc then needs about 40 seconds for a small parser
       and three minutes for C's (1850 lines; -l makes it 1420).
